    title_en: "From IP Address Evaluation to Hardware-Rooted Identity: A Two-Phase Approach to Achieving Sovereign Zero Trust Architecture",
    summary_en: "This article proposes a network security paradigm shift that moves beyond traditional IP address evaluation, aiming to meet increasingly stringent digital sovereignty and data localization requirements. Traditional IP-based security models are costly, lack flexibility, and are prone to lateral movement once breached. The authors suggest adopting a two-phase \"Sovereign Zero Trust\" model. The first phase involves implementing SPIFFE/SPIRE, replacing network boundaries with verifiable, stateful cryptographic identities. This grants every workload a unique, verifiable identity (SVID) and enables mutual authentication via mTLS, fundamentally eliminating reliance on network location. The second phase focuses on combining \"attestation\" and \"geofencing\" to strengthen identity verification, ensuring that data processing adheres to specific geographical boundaries. Practically, this helps industries such as financial services and healthcare automate workload identity management within CI/CD pipelines, significantly reducing the complexity of manual configuration and decreasing reliance on expensive network equipment, thereby accelerating time-to-market.",
